PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Consensus (cols 1,4,6,8,10-12,14,15,18-20 + prior): acrostic Pe+Di+Ca+Re yields verb pedicare; thief must "dabis" (submit to) sodomy/buggery as poena. Supported by grammar, meter, "hortus/fur" comic frame, Catullus 16 parallels, Priapea genre. 

Dissent (cols 2,5,7,9,13,16,17,21): same syllables form noun pedica (iron foot-fetter/shackle); attested Roman agrimensores/Digest penalty for garden theft—thief submits to physical restraint on site. Argues sexual reading is abrupt, requires verb shift, clashes with sober "hac poena culpa luenda" tone; fetter reading preserves literal letters + practical horticultural context.

Key disputes: "dabis" as sexual act vs. tangible object; whether acrostic stops at pedica or forces pedicare; genre tone (obscene pun vs. legal warning). No resolution on attestation of sexual penalty vs. fetters. Acrostic itself undisputed; sexual solution still majority but literal alternative strengthened by historical sources. Unresolved: exact word class & real-world penalty match.}

